From 099e1973abe8484bde91e781b44dbae941ff88f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:00:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 205/209] feat(area): Bake To Brushes

Decision 04's D4, which shipped as a warning that said "not implemented yet".

IDistributorBake.h is the contract: IBakeableComponent, a reflected interface
base deriving from IEditorActionComponent, plus SBakeInstance - a flat POD with
a fixed-size mesh path, a world transform, the render flags and the two ratios.
No STL crosses it and nothing reflected holds a pointer, the same two rules the
shape contract obeys. Any component that generates placed geometry from
parameters can implement it and get the button; today that is the distributor,
which also loses its old reflected ActionButton in favour of the generic
mechanism.

CDistributorBakeTool is a one-shot tool like CShapeRecenterTool, for the same
reason and with the same Display()-not-SetUserData() timing. Inside one CUndo it
takes the object's snapshot, creates one brush per instance through
IEditor::NewObject("Brush", <cgf>) - "Brush" is CBrushObject's class name
(BrushObject.h:175) and its file spec is the .cgf - places each with
CBaseObject::SetWorldTM, names them "<entity>_baked*", then switches the
distributor's own reflected Enabled member off and runs the 1a sync. One Ctrl+Z
therefore removes every brush AND brings the live instances back. The baked
objects are selected at the end.

Two deliberate limits, both logged rather than hidden. The brushes are placed
through the generic CBaseObject API only, because this plugin does not link
EditorQt: CBrushObject's own variables - the render-flag block, view distance,
LOD, material layers - are out of reach, so a baked brush gets a hand-placed
brush's defaults, and the log line names the flags and ratios that were not
carried. And the brushes are created flat with a shared name prefix rather than
inside a CGroup: CGroup lives in EditorQt and its AddMember contract is not
reachable through CBaseObject, and the single undo transaction already does what
grouping was wanted for.

Disabling rather than deleting is the point: the parameters, the seed and the
curve all survive a bake, so switching Enabled back on returns exactly the same
layout.

